\chapter{PEG Combinators and the \code{PEGMatcher}}
\label{ch:peg-combinators}

\section{Two Layers, One Idea}

The definition layer described in \chapref{ch:peg-definition} is a
tokenizer: an ordered set of patterns, tried left to right, with a callback
per match. It is fast and it is predictable, but it cannot express
structure, because a rule is a regular expression subset and a regular
expression subset has no notion of nesting. The combinator layer, which
occupies lines 8886 to 9085 of \code{DSLtk.hpp}, exists to supply that
missing structure. It does so by wrapping the ordinary parser core of
\chapref{ch:parser-core} -- \code{ParsecInput}, \code{Parser},
\code{ExpectedResult} and the operators of Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-core} --
in the three rules that make a grammar a PEG: ordered choice that never
commits, repetition that never commits on partial input, and lookahead that
consumes nothing.

The layer is thin by design. There is no new parser type, no new input
type, and no new error type; every combinator here is a
\code{std::function} wrapped in the existing \code{Parser<T>} and is
indistinguishable, at run time, from a parser you wrote by hand. What
changes is the \emph{discipline} the combinators impose: they commit in the
places a PEG commits, and they backtrack in the places a PEG backtracks.

The layer also contains a second facility that is not a combinator at all:
the streaming \code{PEGMatcher}. Where \code{PEGDefinition::parse} runs to
completion and reports a result, the matcher is a cursor that you advance
one token at a time, with an ordered list of arms that you extend as you go.
It is the right tool for a lexer that has to consult a context -- a mode
flag, a keyword table, a context stack -- that a fixed rule set cannot
express.

\section{The Bridge: \code{as_span_parser}}

Every combinator in this chapter produces either a \code{PEGMatch} or a
container of them, and the reason is that a PEG match is more than a
success: it records the span of text that was consumed, which is what a
lexer needs and what the ordinary \code{Parser<T>} does not provide. The
adapter that supplies this is \code{peg_detail::as_span_parser}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8900--8918. \code{as_span_parser} turns any \code{Parser<T>} into a \code{Parser<PEGMatch>} that reports the span it consumed. The value of the inner parser is discarded.},label={lst:ch26-span}]
template <typename T>
Parser<PEGMatch>
as_span_parser (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser (
      [p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>
        {
          std::size_t begin = in.pos;
          auto r = p (in);
          if (!r)
            return ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>::failure (
                r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
          PEGMatch m;
          m.begin = begin;
          m.end = in.pos;
          m.value = in.get_span (begin);
          return m;
        });
}
\end{lstlisting}

The function lives in the \code{peg_detail} namespace, which is a
naming convention for implementation types rather than a promise of
stability, but it is a public function of a public header, and the shipped
example \code{22-peg-combinators.cpp} does exactly what this section
recommends: it writes its own copy of the adapter under the name
\code{to_span_parser}. A user of the shipped header may do the same, or
may call \code{dsl::peg_detail::as_span_parser} directly.

Two details of the adapter matter. It captures the inner parser by value,
so the result owns a complete copy of the computation and is safe to store
and to call later. And the span it records is the text between the cursor
before the inner parser ran and the cursor after it ran, which for a
successful parse is exactly the matched text; for a failed parse the
adapter forwards the inner error unchanged, including its failure kind.

