\subsection{Sequence}

\code{peg_seq} is variadic and requires at least two arguments. It parses
each argument in turn and produces a single \code{PEGMatch} whose span
covers the whole sequence; the individual values are discarded, which is why
the arguments may have wildly different types.

The interesting part is what happens when a later element fails. The code
rewinds the cursor and upgrades the failure kind:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8935--8945. The failure branch of \code{peg_seq}. A failure after the first element has consumed input rewinds the cursor and marks the failure \code{Committed}.},label={lst:ch26-seq}]
auto rb = b (in);
if (!rb)
  {
    bool consumed = in.pos != begin;
    in.pos = begin;
    auto kind = rb.error.kind;
    if (consumed)
      kind = ParseFailureKind::Committed;
    return ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>::failure (rb.error.pos, kind,
                                              rb.error.expected);
  }
\end{lstlisting}

The cursor is rewound unconditionally, so the input is left exactly as it
was found. But the \code{Committed} kind is a promise to the enclosing
construct: \code{operator|} in the parser core refuses to try its second
alternative when the first one failed with \code{Committed}. That is the
mechanism by which a PEG refuses to backtrack, and it is inherited by
\code{peg_seq} rather than reimplemented.

Note that the first element's failure is passed through unchanged. A
sequence that fails on its first element has consumed nothing, so there is
nothing to commit about, and the failure keeps whatever kind the first
element reported.

The practical consequence is that a sequence is not a safe unit of
backtracking. If you want a sequence to be tried and undone as a unit, you
must wrap it in \code{try_parse}, which resets the kind to \code{Soft} and
restores the cursor. \code{peg_choice} does this for its arguments
automatically.

\subsection{Ordered Choice}

\code{peg_choice} is four lines long:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8960--8969. \code{peg_choice}. Both alternatives are wrapped in \code{try_parse}, so neither can commit.},label={lst:ch26-choice}]
template <typename A, typename B, typename... Rest>
auto
peg_choice (const Parser<A> &a, const Parser<B> &b, const Rest &...rest)
{
  auto ab = try_parse (a) | try_parse (b);
  if constexpr (sizeof...(rest) == 0)
    return ab;
  else
    return peg_choice (ab, rest...);
}
\end{lstlisting}

Because \code{try_parse} restores the cursor and downgrades a failure to
\code{Soft}, the \code{|} operator always tries the second alternative when
the first one fails, whatever the first one did. This is the difference
between a PEG and a backtracking regular expression matcher, and it is
enforced here by construction rather than by convention.

There is a constraint that is easy to trip over. \code{operator|} in the
parser core is declared as \code{template <typename T> Parser<T> operator|
(const Parser<T> &, const Parser<T> &)}, so both operands must have the
\emph{same} value type. Since \code{peg_choice} is built on \code{|}, its
two arguments must also have the same type, and a call such as
\code{peg_choice(digit, as_span_parser(alpha))} does not compile: the
error is a deduction failure inside \code{operator|}, and it names the
mismatch in its message. The fix is to convert both alternatives to the same
type first, which is why the shipped example converts both sides to
\code{Parser<PEGMatch>} before offering them to \code{peg_choice}.

\subsection{Repetition and Optionality}

\code{peg_opt}, \code{peg_many} and \code{peg_many1} are one-liners
built on the primitives of the parser core:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8971--8993. The three repetition combinators.},label={lst:ch26-rep}]
template <typename T>
auto
peg_opt (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return optional (try_parse (p));
}

template <typename T>
auto
peg_many (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return *try_parse (p);
}

template <typename T>
auto
peg_many1 (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return p & *try_parse (p);
}
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{try_parse} wrapper is what makes the repetition backtracking.
The primitive repetition operator of the parser core stops at the first
failure and restores the cursor, but it gives up entirely -- and reports a
failure -- if the inner parser failed with \code{Committed}. Wrapping the
inner parser in \code{try_parse} downgrades every failure to
\code{Soft}, so the loop always terminates cleanly with the characters it
has consumed so far.

\code{peg_opt} uses \code{optional}, which returns an empty
\code{std::optional} rather than a failure when the inner parser fails.
Combined with \code{try_parse}, the result never fails and never commits,
which is exactly the behaviour of the optional operator in a PEG.

\code{peg_many1} deserves a note about its shape. It is
\code{p & *try_parse(p)}, and the \code{&} of the parser core is a
\emph{sequence}, not a lookahead: it parses \code{p}, then parses the
repetition, and produces a pair. So the result of \code{peg_many1} is a
pair whose first element is the first iteration and whose second is a vector
of the remaining ones -- an awkward shape, but a faithful one, since the
PEG combinator layer is a thin wrapper and does not reshape its results.

\subsection{Lookahead}

\code{peg_and} and \code{peg_not} are the two lookahead operators. Both
save the cursor, run the inner parser, restore the cursor, and produce a
\code{bool}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8996--9028. \code{peg_and} and \code{peg_not}. The cursor is saved and restored in both cases, so neither consumes input.},label={lst:ch26-look}]
template <typename T>
auto
peg_and (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser (
      [p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<bool>
        {
          auto save = in.pos;
          auto r = p (in);
          in.pos = save;
          if (!r)
            return ExpectedResult<bool>::failure (
                r.error.pos, ParseFailureKind::Soft, r.error.expected);
          return true;
        });
}

template <typename T>
auto
peg_not (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser (
      [p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<bool>
        {
          auto save = in.pos;
          auto r = p (in);
          in.pos = save;
          if (r)
            return fail_expected<bool> (in, "negative lookahead");
          return true;
        });
}
\end{lstlisting}

Two observations. The failure of \code{peg_and} is always \code{Soft}, so a
lookahead never commits; and the failure of \code{peg_not} carries the
label \code{"negative lookahead"}, which is the string that appears in the
diagnostics when a negative lookahead rejects the input. Note also that
\code{peg_not} uses \code{fail_expected}, which reports the failure at
the \emph{current} cursor -- the restored one -- rather than at the position
where the inner parser stopped.

\subsection{Adapting Rules and Relabelling Channels}

\code{peg_rule} connects the two layers. It takes a \code{PEGRule} from a
definition and produces an ordinary parser:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 9031--9052. \code{peg_rule}. It honours \code{must_fail} because it goes through \code{try_match}, and it fills in the channel, the ignored flag and the source position.},label={lst:ch26-pegrule}]
inline Parser<PEGMatch>
peg_rule (const PEGRule &rule)
{
  return parser (
      [&rule] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>
        {
          std::size_t begin = in.pos;
          auto end = rule.try_match (in.source, begin);
          if (!end)
            return fail_expected<PEGMatch> (in, rule.pattern_text);
          in.pos = *end;
          PEGMatch m;
          m.begin = begin;
          m.end = *end;
          m.value = in.source.substr (begin, *end - begin);
          m.rule = const_cast<PEGRule *> (&rule);
          m.channel = rule.channel;
          m.ignored = rule.channel.is_ignore ();
          peg_detail::line_col_at (in.source, begin, m.line, m.column);
          return m;
        });
}
\end{lstlisting}

The rule's own semantic action is \emph{not} run: \code{peg_rule} reports
a match and leaves the action to the caller. This is a real difference from
the definition layer, where the action fires automatically, and it is worth
being explicit about because the two layers are otherwise so similar.

\code{peg_channel} is the other adapter. It takes a parser and relabels
the channel of the match it produces:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 9055--9085. \code{peg_channel} and its name-taking overload.},label={lst:ch26-channel}]
template <typename T>
auto
peg_channel (PEGChannel channel, const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser (
      [channel = std::move (channel),
       p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>
        {
          std::size_t begin = in.pos;
          auto r = p (in);
          if (!r)
            return ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>::failure (
                r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
          PEGMatch m;
          m.begin = begin;
          m.end = in.pos;
          m.value = in.get_span (begin);
          m.channel = channel;
          m.ignored = channel.is_ignore ();
          peg_detail::line_col_at (in.source, begin, m.line, m.column);
          return m;
        });
}

template <typename T>
auto
peg_channel (std::string_view name, const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return peg_channel (new_peg_channel (name), p);
}
\end{lstlisting}

The second overload goes through \code{new_peg_channel}, so it inherits
that function's validation: a name that does not begin with \code{@} makes
the call throw \code{std::invalid_argument}, at the point where the
combinator is built rather than where it is run. Since the channel is
captured by value in the returned parser, the exception is raised once, when
the parser is constructed.

Note also what \code{peg_channel} does \emph{not} carry over. The match it
produces has a fresh \code{PEGMatch} with only the span, the value, the
channel, the ignored flag and the position filled in; the \code{rule}
pointer is null, because the channel adapter has no rule to point at.

\section{A Small Expression Parser}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch26-expr} builds an expression parser out of the
combinators. The grammar is the usual one:

\begin{center}
\code{expr := term (('+' | '-') term)*}\qquad
\code{term := number | identifier}\qquad
\code{number := [0-9]+}\qquad
\code{identifier := [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*}
\end{center}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={An expression parser built from the PEG combinators. Note that both alternatives of \code{peg_choice} are converted to \code{Parser<PEGMatch>} before they are offered to it.},label={lst:ch26-expr}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

namespace
{
/// Wrap any Parser<T> so that it reports the span it consumed.
dsl::Parser<dsl::PEGMatch>
spanned (const auto &inner)
{
  return dsl::peg_detail::as_span_parser (inner);
}

void
show (char const *label, dsl::ParseOutcome<dsl::PEGMatch> const &r)
{
  std::cout << label << " -> ";
  if (r.value)
    std::cout << "\"" << r.value->value << "\"";
  else
    {
      std::cout << "fail at offset " << r.error.pos << ", expected";
      for (auto const &e : r.error.expected)
        std::cout << " \"" << e << "\"";
    }
  std::cout << "\n";
}
} // namespace

int
main ()
{
  auto digit
      = dsl::satisfy ([] (char c) { return std::isdigit ((unsigned char) c) != 0; },
                      "digit");
  auto alpha
      = dsl::satisfy ([] (char c) { return std::isalpha ((unsigned char) c) != 0; },
                      "alpha");
  auto alnum
      = dsl::satisfy ([] (char c) { return std::isalnum ((unsigned char) c) != 0; },
                      "alnum");

  // number := [0-9]+
  auto number = spanned (dsl::peg_many1 (digit));

  // identifier := [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*
  auto identifier = dsl::peg_seq (dsl::peg_and (alpha), dsl::peg_many1 (alnum));

  // term := number | identifier      (ordered choice, same value type)
  auto term = dsl::peg_choice (number, identifier);

  // expr := term (('+' | '-') term)*
  auto plus_or_minus = dsl::peg_choice (dsl::ch ('+'), dsl::ch ('-'));
  auto expr = dsl::peg_seq (term, dsl::peg_many (dsl::peg_seq (plus_or_minus,
                                                            term)));

  show ("expr \"1+2\"", dsl::run_parser (expr, "1+2"));
  show ("expr \"x-3\"", dsl::run_parser (expr, "x-3"));
  show ("expr \"1+\"", dsl::run_parser (expr, "1+"));
  show ("expr \"1 2\"", dsl::run_parser (expr, "1 2"));

  // optional sign: a? never fails and never commits
  auto signed_number = dsl::peg_seq (dsl::peg_opt (dsl::ch ('+')),
                                     dsl::peg_many1 (digit));
  show ("signed \"+7\"", dsl::run_parser (signed_number, "+7"));
  show ("signed \"7\"", dsl::run_parser (signed_number, "7"));

  // negative lookahead: !'-' fails softly when a minus follows
  auto unsigned_number = dsl::peg_seq (dsl::peg_not (dsl::ch ('-')),
                                       dsl::peg_many1 (digit));
  show ("unsigned \"7\"", dsl::run_parser (unsigned_number, "7"));
  show ("unsigned \"-7\"", dsl::run_parser (unsigned_number, "-7"));

  // peg_channel relabels the match
  auto tagged = dsl::peg_channel ("@NUM", number);
  auto tagged_result = dsl::run_parser (tagged, "42");
  if (tagged_result.value)
    std::cout << "tagged \"42\" -> channel " << tagged_result.value->channel.name
              << ", ignored " << std::boolalpha << tagged_result.value->ignored
              << "\n";

  // the name-taking overload goes through new_peg_channel and validates
  try
    {
      (void) dsl::peg_channel ("NUM", number);
    }
  catch (const std::invalid_argument &e)
    {
      std::cout << "peg_channel(\"NUM\", ...) throws: " << e.what () << "\n";
    }

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch26-expr}.},label={lst:ch26-expr-out}]
expr "1+2" -> "1+2"
expr "x-3" -> "x-3"
expr "1+" -> fail at offset 1, expected "<end-of-input>"
expr "1 2" -> fail at offset 1, expected "<end-of-input>"
signed "+7" -> "+7"
signed "7" -> "7"
unsigned "7" -> "7"
unsigned "-7" -> fail at offset 0, expected "negative lookahead"
tagged "42" -> channel @NUM, ignored false
peg_channel("NUM", ...) throws: dsl::new_peg_channel: channel names must begin with '@'
\end{lstlisting}

The two failing expressions are instructive. \code{"1+"} fails at offset one
rather than at offset two: the inner repetition tried \code{peg_seq(op,
term)}, the operator consumed the plus, the term failed, and because
\code{peg_many} wraps its argument in \code{try_parse} the partial attempt
was undone -- the cursor went back to offset one -- and the repetition
simply stopped. The expression parser had therefore matched \code{"1"} and
left the plus sign as trailing input, which \code{run_parser} reports as
an expectation of end-of-input. This is exactly the behaviour a PEG should
have, and it is very different from the behaviour of a backtracking regular
expression, which would have reported the failure at the plus sign.

The negative lookahead failure is reported with the label
\code{"negative lookahead"} rather than with the pattern that was rejected,
which is a small loss of diagnostic detail that is worth knowing about when
you are reading an error message.

\section{The Streaming Matcher}

\code{PEGMatcher} is the second facility in this layer, and it is not a
combinator at all. It is a cursor over the input with an ordered list of
arms, and it is created by \code{peg_new_matcher}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8877--8884. \code{peg_new_matcher} binds a definition and a source text into a matcher.},label={lst:ch26-newmatcher}]
inline PEGMatcher
peg_new_matcher (const PEGDefinition &peg, std::string_view text)
{
  PEGMatcher m;
  m.def = &peg;
  m.text = text;
  return m;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The public surface is small. \code{is_spent()} reports whether the cursor
has reached the end of the text, \code{position()} reports the offset, and
\code{close()} is a no-op that exists for symmetry with an input handle.
Three methods filter by channel: \code{only(name)} restricts matching to a
single channel, \code{include(name)} adds a channel to the accepted set,
and \code{exclude(name)} rejects a channel; \code{channel_allowed(name)}
asks about the current filter. \code{wildcard(fn)} builds an arm that fires
when no rule arm matches, and \code{operator<<} pushes an arm and tries it.

The semantics of \code{operator<<} are the part that surprises people, so
they are worth stating before anything else:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8750--8756. \code{operator<<} pushes the arm and immediately calls \code{step}. The arm list is cleared when an arm fires.},label={lst:ch26-opshl}]
PEGMatcher &
operator<< (const peg_detail::PEGArm &arm)
{
  arms_.push_back (arm);
  step ();
  return *this;
}
\end{lstlisting}

Pushing an arm runs the step immediately, and a step that fires clears the
whole arm list. A chain of \code{<<} operators therefore fires \emph{as many
times as there are matching alternatives}, not once. The shipped example
\code{24-peg-matcher.cpp} writes

\begin{center}
\code{matcher << keyword(cb) << number(cb) << ident(cb) << plus(cb) <<
matcher.wildcard(cb);}
\end{center}

inside its loop. Two independent faults combine there, and it is worth
separating them, because they are of quite different kinds.

The first is the eager firing. Each push fires as soon as it can, so with
sound rule pointers the keyword arm would fire at the first token, the
identifier arm at the second, and the wildcard arm would fire before the
number arm ever got a chance: the number \code{10} would be split into two
wildcards. The fix for this is not to change the matcher but to change the
loop -- push one arm at a time and stop as soon as the cursor advances.

The second is the dangling rule pointer. The example holds
\code{PEGRule} references across further \code{add_rule} calls, so by the
time the arms are built the references no longer designate the rules they
were taken from; see \chapref{ch:peg-definition}. With those pointers, no
rule arm ever matches, and every character of the input falls through to the
wildcard arm. The example therefore prints a string of unknown characters
rather than a token stream.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch26-matcher} is a matcher written the right way. It keeps
the arms in a vector of its own, it takes the rule pointers only after the
grammar is complete, and it pushes them one at a time, checking after each
push whether the cursor moved.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A streaming matcher. The arms are pushed one at a time, and the loop stops as soon as one of them advances the cursor.},label={lst:ch26-matcher}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int
main ()
{
  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  // Every rule is added, and configured, before any pointer to it is
  // taken: add_rule returns a reference into a std::vector, and the next
  // add_rule may reallocate it.
  {
    auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \t\n]+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
    ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
  }
  {
    auto &kw = grammar.add_rule<"print"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
    kw.channel = dsl::new_peg_channel ("@KEYWORD");
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
  }

  // The grammar is frozen: from here on the pointers are stable.
  auto visible = grammar.rules ();
  auto find = [&visible] (std::string_view pattern) -> const dsl::PEGRule *
  {
    for (const dsl::PEGRule *r : visible)
      if (r->pattern_text == pattern)
        return r;
    return nullptr;
  };

  auto source = std::string{ "print x+10 if_bad" };

  auto emit = [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
  { std::cout << "[" << m.value << ":" << m.channel.name << "]"; };

  // Arms in priority order; the wildcard is the last resort.
  std::vector<dsl::peg_detail::PEGArm> arms;
  arms.push_back ((*find ("print")) (emit));
  arms.push_back ((*find ("[0-9]+")) (emit));
  arms.push_back ((*find ("[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*")) (emit));
  arms.push_back ((*find ("+")) (emit));

  auto matcher = dsl::peg_new_matcher (grammar, source);
  auto wildcard_arm = matcher.wildcard (emit);

  std::cout << "unfiltered:\n";
  while (!matcher.is_spent ())
    {
      auto before = matcher.position ();
      for (auto &arm : arms)
        {
          matcher << arm;
          if (matcher.position () != before)
            break;
        }
      if (matcher.position () == before)
        matcher << wildcard_arm;
    }
  matcher.close ();
  std::cout << "\nposition " << matcher.position () << " of " << source.size ()
            << "\n";

  // The same grammar, but with the @KEYWORD channel filtered out: the
  // word "print" now falls through to the identifier rule.
  auto filtered = dsl::peg_new_matcher (grammar, source);
  filtered.exclude ("@KEYWORD");
  std::cout << "\n@KEYWORD allowed? " << std::boolalpha
            << filtered.channel_allowed ("@KEYWORD") << "\nfiltered:\n";
  while (!filtered.is_spent ())
    {
      auto before = filtered.position ();
      for (auto &arm : arms)
        {
          filtered << arm;
          if (filtered.position () != before)
            break;
        }
      if (filtered.position () == before)
        filtered << filtered.wildcard (emit);
    }
  std::cout << "\n";

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch26-matcher}. With the keyword channel excluded, the word "print" is lexed by the identifier rule instead.},label={lst:ch26-matcher-out}]
unfiltered:
[print:@KEYWORD][x:@DEFAULT][+:@DEFAULT][10:@DEFAULT][if_bad:@DEFAULT]
position 17 of 17

@KEYWORD allowed? false
filtered:
[print:@DEFAULT][x:@DEFAULT][+:@DEFAULT][10:@DEFAULT][if_bad:@DEFAULT]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{The Step}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch26-step} is the step function, and it is the heart of the
matcher.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8760--8820. \code{PEGMatcher::step}. Ignored-channel matches are skipped first; then the arms are tried in order; then the wildcard arms; and if nothing fires the arms are kept for a later push.},label={lst:ch26-step}]
void
step ()
{
  if (arms_.empty () || is_spent ())
    return;
  skip_ignored ();
  if (is_spent ())
    {
      arms_.clear ();
      return;
    }
  // Non-wildcard arms in order: first match wins.
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < arms_.size (); ++i)
    {
      const auto &arm = arms_[i];
      if (arm.is_wildcard)
        continue;
      if (arm.is_channel)
        {
          for (const PEGRule *r : def->rules ())
            {
              if (r->channel.name != arm.channel.name)
                continue;
              if (auto end = r->try_match (text, pos))
                {
                  fire (arm.action, r, pos, *end);
                  arms_.clear ();
                  return;
                }
            }
          continue;
        }
      if (!arm.rule || !channel_allowed (arm.rule->channel.name))
        continue;
      if (auto end = arm.rule->try_match (text, pos))
        {
          fire (arm.action, arm.rule, pos, *end);
          arms_.clear ();
          return;
        }
    }
  // No rule arm matched: fire the first wildcard arm, if any.
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < arms_.size (); ++i)
    {
      if (!arms_[i].is_wildcard)
        continue;
      PEGMatch m;
      m.value = text.substr (pos, 1);
      m.begin = pos;
      m.end = pos + 1;
      m.rule = nullptr;
      peg_detail::line_col_at (text, pos, m.line, m.column);
      if (arms_[i].action)
        arms_[i].action (m);
      ++pos; // wildcard consumes one character
      arms_.clear ();
      return;
    }
  // Nothing fired yet: keep the accumulated arms; the next << may
  // provide one that matches at this position.
}
\end{lstlisting}

Figure~\ref{fig:ch26-step} draws the same flow.

\begin{pseudocode}[One streaming matching step]
\textbf{while} an ignored rule consumes input, advance without actions\\
\textbf{for each} eligible non-wildcard arm in registration order\\
\quad \textbf{if} its rule matches, advance, fire its action, clear arms, return\\
\textbf{if} a wildcard exists, consume one byte, fire, clear arms, return\\
Otherwise retain the arms and cursor for the next insertion
\end{pseudocode}

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{dslgraph}
  \node[dslbox, text width=40mm] (arms) {the arm list: ordered, accumulated};
  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of arms, text width=40mm] (skip) {\texttt{skip\_ignored()}: consume\\\texttt{@IGNORE} matches silently};
  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of skip] (loop) {for each arm, in order};
  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of loop, text width=38mm] (how) {a channel arm scans the rules of the definition with that channel; a rule arm consults \texttt{arm.rule->try\_match}};
  \node[dsldecision, below=13mm of how] (ok) {did it match?};
  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of ok] (fire) {\texttt{fire()}: build the match,\\\texttt{pos = end}, run the action};
  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of fire] (wild) {no rule arm matched: fire the first\\\texttt{wildcard} arm, consume one character};
  \node[dslterm, below=11mm of wild] (keep) {nothing fired: keep the arms\\\texttt{pos} is unchanged};

  \draw[dsledge] (arms) -- (skip);
  \draw[dsledge] (skip) -- (loop);
  \draw[dsledge] (loop) -- (how);
  \draw[dsledge] (how) -- (ok);
