Four details of the step are worth drawing out.

The first is that the ignored channel is handled before any arm is
consulted. \code{skip_ignored()} walks the rules of the definition and
consumes any \code{@IGNORE} match, repeatedly, until it makes no progress.
The semantic actions of those rules are \emph{not} run, which is a
difference from the definition layer, where an ignored match is delivered
to the action like any other. The matcher treats \code{@IGNORE} as an
instruction; the definition layer treats it as a label.

The second is that a rule arm is subject to the channel filter but a
channel arm is not. A rule arm is skipped when
\code{channel_allowed(arm.rule->channel.name)} is false; a channel arm is
consulted regardless, and it searches the rules of the definition for one
whose channel name matches. A channel arm is therefore a way of saying "any
rule on this channel", and it is not affected by the filters.

The third is the fallback. When no rule arm matched, the first wildcard arm
fires and consumes exactly one character. The match it produces has a null
\code{rule} pointer and the default channel, so a consumer can tell a
wildcard match from a rule match by looking at \code{m.rule}. A wildcard arm
is what guarantees that the matcher makes progress; without one, a matcher
that reaches a position no rule accepts will spin in the caller's loop
forever, because the arms are retained rather than discarded.

The fourth is that the arm list is retained when nothing fires. This is
what makes it possible to build the arm list incrementally: you may push a
rule arm, discover that it does not match here, push another, and have both
consulted at the same position.

\subsection{The Channel Filter}

The filter is implemented with a mode and a set of names:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8696--8733. The channel filter of the matcher. Note that \code{include} on a fresh matcher leaves the mode at \code{All}, so it has no effect until an \code{exclude} has switched the mode to \code{Exclude}.},label={lst:ch26-filter}]
PEGMatcher &
only (std::string_view channel)
{
  mode_ = FilterMode::Only;
  channels_.clear ();
  channels_.push_back (std::string (channel));
  return *this;
}

PEGMatcher &
include (std::string_view channel)
{
  if (mode_ == FilterMode::Exclude)
    mode_ = FilterMode::Only;
  channels_.push_back (std::string (channel));
  return *this;
}

PEGMatcher &
exclude (std::string_view channel)
{
  if (mode_ != FilterMode::Exclude)
    {
      mode_ = FilterMode::Exclude;
      channels_.clear ();
    }
  channels_.push_back (std::string (channel));
  return *this;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The behaviour that falls out of this code is not what the names suggest, and
a user who filters by trial and error will lose time to it. On a fresh
matcher the mode is \code{All}, so \code{channel_allowed} returns true for
every name. \code{only(c)} clears the set and switches to \code{Only},
giving exactly one accepted channel. \code{exclude(c)} clears the set and
switches to \code{Exclude}, giving everything but \code{c}. But
\code{include(c)} on a fresh matcher merely appends a name to a set that
the \code{All} mode ignores, so it changes nothing at all: the matcher
still accepts every channel. And \code{exclude(c)} followed by
\code{include(d)} switches the mode to \code{Only} while leaving \code{c} in
the set, so the result accepts \emph{both} \code{c} and \code{d} -- the
opposite of what a reader would expect.

The safe spells are therefore \code{only(c)} for a single channel and
\code{exclude(c)} for a blacklist, and the code of
Listing~\ref{lst:ch26-matcher} uses the second of these.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\paragraph{\code{operator<<} fires eagerly.} This is the sharpest edge in
the layer and the one most likely to be encountered, because the natural
spelling of an ordered choice is a chain of \code{<<}. The chain fires once
per matching alternative rather than once per token. The correct idiom is
to push one arm at a time and check the cursor, as in
Listing~\ref{lst:ch26-matcher}.

\paragraph{The shipped matcher example does not work.} As described above,
\code{24-peg-matcher.cpp} produces a stream of unknown characters
rather than tokens. Two independent faults combine: the example holds
\code{PEGRule} references across further \code{add_rule} calls, which
invalidates them (see \chapref{ch:peg-definition}), and it chains the
arms, which fires them eagerly. The example compiles, runs, and reports
nothing wrong.

\paragraph{\code{peg_choice} requires both alternatives to have the same
value type.} This is a consequence of \code{operator|} in the parser core
and it is a compile-time error, so it is cheap to discover, but the
diagnostic is a template deduction failure rather than a helpful message.

\paragraph{A sequence commits.} \code{peg_seq} marks a failure as
\code{Committed} when the failure comes after some input has been
consumed, and a committed failure prevents \code{operator|} from trying the
next alternative. If you write a sequence directly as an alternative of a
raw \code{|}, the second alternative will never be tried. Use
\code{peg_choice}, which wraps both sides in \code{try_parse}, or wrap
the sequence yourself.

\paragraph{Repetition is greedy and does not backtrack within an iteration.}
\code{peg_many} takes as many iterations as it can. A repetition whose
body can match several lengths -- a nested repetition, say -- will take the
first length that lets the following element succeed, and will not try a
shorter one. This is the standard PEG behaviour and it is the reason PEG
grammars are not equivalent to regular expressions.

\paragraph{The combinator layer produces spans, not trees.} Every
combinator in this layer returns a \code{PEGMatch} or a container of them.
None of them builds a tree; the tree has to be assembled from the matches
afterwards, or by a semantic action. That is the subject of
\chapref{ch:peg-semantic-values}.

\paragraph{\code{peg_rule} does not fire the rule's action.} A rule
adapted into a parser reports a match and stops. If you want the action to
run, you must run it yourself, which is another reason to prefer writing
the combinator directly over adapting a rule.

\paragraph{\code{peg_channel} discards the rule pointer.} The match it
produces has a null \code{rule}, so a consumer cannot tell which rule
matched, only which channel the match was assigned to.

\paragraph{There is no "match the whole string" entry point.} The two ways
to run a PEG over a text are \code{PEGDefinition::parse(text)}, which runs
to completion, and \code{peg_new_matcher}, which is the streaming cursor
described above. Neither the combinator layer nor the matcher offers a
function that parses an entire string and reports whether it matched, which
is a surprising omission given how natural such a function would be; the
closest is \code{run_parser} from the parser core, which requires an
ordinary \code{Parser<T>} and therefore rejects trailing input.

\paragraph{The matcher never reports an error.} When no arm fires and there
is no wildcard arm, the matcher simply stops advancing. There is no error
position, no message and no failure flag; the caller has to notice that the
cursor did not move and decide what to do.

\section{Closing}

The combinator layer is where the PEG idea becomes usable. A sequence,
\code{peg_seq}, gives you a span. An ordered choice, \code{peg_choice},
never commits. A lookahead, \code{peg_and} or \code{peg_not}, consumes
nothing. A repetition, \code{peg_many}, undoes a partial iteration. All of
them are built on the parser core, so a PEG grammar and an ordinary
combinator grammar can be mixed freely in one program.

The streaming matcher is the other half of the layer, and it is the right
tool when the set of rules depends on context: a keyword table that changes,
a mode flag, an indentation stack. It is also the part of the layer with
the most surprising semantics, because pushing an arm fires it immediately
and the arm list is cleared when it does.

What none of this layer provides is a tree. Both layers hand you matches
and spans; assembling a syntax tree from them is a separate design problem,
and it is the problem that \chapref{ch:peg-semantic-values} takes up.
